  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{4}\\frac{1}{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{6}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2 \\\\ {\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-8+4 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
